title: The night changes the voice
story: fernhill
description: The ADR-250 phrasebook probe. The weathervane answers three ways —
  a line only books provide, a line no book may beat, and a line only the
  default book covers — before and after the story turns to midnight.
seed: 1

---

# EVENING. `evening-voice` has no `while` clause, so it is the default book and
# is active now. Its `vane-mood` is `, first-time`, and this is the first time.
> examine the weathervane
[OK: contains "The vane noses the evening breeze, unhurried"]

# Second look, same book: the alternative after the `or`.
> examine the weathervane
[OK: contains "The vane sits easy in the last of the light"]
[OK: not contains "noses the evening breeze"]

# Twelve turns to reach turn 14. `wait` is a real turn; `score` and `inventory`
# are not, which is worth knowing before counting turns for a sequence.
> wait
[OK: contains "Time passes"]

> wait
[OK: contains "Time passes"]

> wait
[OK: contains "Time passes"]

> wait
[OK: contains "Time passes"]

> wait
[OK: contains "Time passes"]

> wait
[OK: contains "Time passes"]

> wait
[OK: contains "Time passes"]

> wait
[OK: contains "Time passes"]

> wait
[OK: contains "Time passes"]

> wait
[OK: contains "Time passes"]

> wait
[OK: contains "Time passes"]

# Turn 14: `define sequence the long night / at turn 14 / phrase dusk-deepens /
# change the story to midnight`. The story state flips here, and with it the
# active phrasebook.
> wait
[OK: contains "Somewhere beyond the grounds the last light goes out of the sky"]
[OK: contains "Midnight is coming on"]

# MIDNIGHT. `midnight-voice while midnight` now wins `vane-mood`, and it has
# its OWN first-time counter — the evening book already spent its first-time
# above, and that has no effect on this one.
> examine the weathervane
[OK: contains "The vane swings hard north, as if the night had opinions"]

> examine the weathervane
[OK: contains "The vane holds north. Of course it does"]

# `vane-story` is story text. Both books define a line for it, and both lose,
# in both states — compare arrival.transcript, which asserts the identical
# sentence during evening.
> push the weathervane
[OK: contains "creaks the same one story it always tells, whatever the hour"]
[OK: not contains "A book must never win this line"]
[OK: not contains "Nor this one"]

# `vane-quiet` appears only in `evening-voice`. At midnight the midnight book
# has no entry for it, and the line still arrives — the default book is
# consulted for keys the active book does not carry, rather than being
# replaced by it.
> touch the weathervane
[OK: contains "The vane keeps its own counsel"]
